[
{ch:19,q:"What is a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid?",choices:["A proton (H<sup>+</sup>) donor","A proton (H<sup>+</sup>) acceptor","An electron-pair donor","A substance that releases OH<sup>&minus;</sup> in water"],explain:"A Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid is defined as a proton (H<sup>+</sup>) donor."},
{ch:19,q:"What is a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry base?",choices:["A proton (H<sup>+</sup>) acceptor","A proton (H<sup>+</sup>) donor","An electron-pair acceptor","A substance that always contains OH<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"A Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry base is defined as a proton (H<sup>+</sup>) acceptor."},
{ch:19,q:"Why can H<sup>+</sup> and &ldquo;a proton&rdquo; be used interchangeably?",choices:["A hydrogen atom has just one proton and one electron, so ionizing by losing its electron leaves only the proton","A hydrogen atom has two protons and one electron","H<sup>+</sup> is actually a neutron with a positive charge","Protons and electrons have identical mass, so they are indistinguishable"],explain:"A hydrogen atom contains one proton and one electron; losing the electron to form H<sup>+</sup> leaves only the bare proton, so the two terms are interchangeable."},
{ch:19,q:"In aqueous solution, a proton is most accurately represented as:",choices:["H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq), though H<sup>+</sup>(aq) is used as a convenient simplification","OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) exclusively","H<sub>2</sub>(aq)","A free, unsolvated H<sup>+</sup> ion with no water molecule attached"],explain:"The true form of a proton in water is H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq) (hydroxonium/oxonium/hydronium ion), though H<sup>+</sup>(aq) is used as a simplification."},
{ch:19,q:"In the reaction HA + B &harr; A<sup>&minus;</sup> + BH<sup>+</sup>, what are the two conjugate acid&ndash;base pairs?",choices:["HA/A<sup>&minus;</sup> and B/BH<sup>+</sup>","HA/B and A<sup>&minus;</sup>/BH<sup>+</sup>","HA/BH<sup>+</sup> and A<sup>&minus;</sup>/B","HA/A<sup>&minus;</sup> and BH<sup>+</sup>/B, which are identical pairs"],explain:"The conjugate pairs are species differing by one proton: HA/A<sup>&minus;</sup> and B/BH<sup>+</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"How is the conjugate base of an acid obtained?",choices:["Remove one H<sup>+</sup> from the acid and reduce its charge by 1","Add one H<sup>+</sup> to the acid and increase its charge by 1","Remove one OH<sup>&minus;</sup> from the acid","Double the charge on the acid"],explain:"To write the conjugate base of an acid, remove one H<sup>+</sup> and reduce the charge by 1."},
{ch:19,q:"How is the conjugate acid of a base obtained?",choices:["Add one H<sup>+</sup> to the base and increase its charge by 1","Remove one H<sup>+</sup> from the base and reduce its charge by 1","Add one OH<sup>&minus;</sup> to the base","Halve the charge on the base"],explain:"To write the conjugate acid of a base, add one H<sup>+</sup> and increase the charge by 1."},
{ch:19,q:"What is the conjugate base of H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>","CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>","H<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>","CO<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"Removing one H<sup>+</sup> from H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> gives its conjugate base, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"What is the conjugate acid of CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>?",choices:["HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>","H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>","CO<sub>2</sub>","OH<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"Adding one H<sup>+</sup> to CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> gives its conjugate acid, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"In CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq), which species acts as the base?",choices:["H<sub>2</sub>O, since it accepts a proton to form H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>","CH<sub>3</sub>COOH, since it is written first","CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>, since it is negatively charged","H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>, since it contains three hydrogens"],explain:"Water accepts a proton (H<sup>+</sup>) from ethanoic acid to become H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>, so water is acting as the Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry base here."},
{ch:19,q:"Why does the ammonium ion, NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>, act as a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid rather than a base?",choices:["It cannot accept another H<sup>+</sup>, so instead it loses H<sup>+</sup> to form its conjugate base NH<sub>3</sub>","It has no hydrogen atoms available to lose","It always remains fully undissociated in water","It contains a negative charge that repels protons"],explain:"NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> cannot accept another proton, so instead it donates H<sup>+</sup>, acting as a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid and forming its conjugate base, NH<sub>3</sub>."},
{ch:19,q:"What is the key difference between a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry base and an alkali?",choices:["A base is any proton acceptor; an alkali is specifically a soluble base that releases OH<sup>&minus;</sup> in water &ndash; every alkali is a base, but not every base is an alkali","An alkali is any proton acceptor, while a base is restricted to hydroxides only","A base and an alkali are entirely different, unrelated concepts","Every base is an alkali, but not every alkali is a base"],explain:"A base is any species that can accept a proton; an alkali is a smaller, specific group of soluble bases that release OH<sup>&minus;</sup> in water. All alkalis are bases, but not all bases are alkalis."},
{ch:19,q:"What does it mean for a species to be amphiprotic?",choices:["It can act as both a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid and a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry base","It can never participate in acid&ndash;base reactions","It must always be a solid at room temperature","It can only ever act as a base, never as an acid"],explain:"An amphiprotic species can act as both a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid and a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry base, depending on the reaction."},
{ch:19,q:"What two structural features must an amphiprotic species possess?",choices:["A releasable H (to act as an acid) and a lone pair of electrons (to act as a base)","Two separate negative charges","Only a lone pair of electrons, with no releasable H","Only a releasable H, with no lone pair"],explain:"To act as an acid it must be able to release H<sup>+</sup>, and to act as a base it must have a lone pair able to accept H<sup>+</sup>; an amphiprotic species needs both."},
{ch:19,q:"Write the equation showing HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> acting as an acid.",choices:["HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq)","HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)","HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &harr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)","HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &harr; CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)"],explain:"Acting as an acid, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> donates a proton to water, forming CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> and H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"Write the equation showing HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> acting as a base.",choices:["HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)","HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq)","HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq)","HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &harr; CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)"],explain:"Acting as a base, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> accepts a proton from water, forming H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> and OH<sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"What is the difference between amphiprotic and amphoteric?",choices:["Amphiprotic refers specifically to Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry proton transfer; amphoteric is broader and includes acid&ndash;base behaviour with no proton transfer &ndash; all amphiprotic substances are amphoteric, but not vice versa","Amphoteric refers specifically to proton transfer, while amphiprotic is the broader term","The two terms are completely unrelated in meaning","Amphiprotic substances can never be amphoteric"],explain:"Amphiprotic specifically concerns proton transfer; amphoteric is broader, including acid&ndash;base behaviour explained by Lewis theory with no proton transfer. All amphiprotic substances are amphoteric, but not all amphoteric substances are amphiprotic."},
{ch:19,q:"Why is aluminium oxide described as amphoteric but not amphiprotic?",choices:["It reacts with both acids and alkalis, but this behaviour does not involve proton transfer in the Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry sense","It has no acid&ndash;base behaviour of any kind","It only reacts with acids, never with alkalis","It is a strong Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid only"],explain:"Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and bases (amphoteric), but this is not explained by proton donation/acceptance, so it is not amphiprotic."},
{ch:19,q:"How is pH defined?",choices:["pH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>[H<sup>+</sup>]","pH = log<sub>10</sub>[H<sup>+</sup>]","pH = [H<sup>+</sup>] / 14","pH = 10<sup>[H<sup>+</sup>]</sup>"],explain:"pH is defined as the negative base-10 logarithm of [H<sup>+</sup>]: pH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>[H<sup>+</sup>]."},
{ch:19,q:"How is [H<sup>+</sup>] calculated from a given pH value?",choices:["[H<sup>+</sup>] = 10<sup>&minus;pH</sup>","[H<sup>+</sup>] = 10<sup>pH</sup>","[H<sup>+</sup>] = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(pH)","[H<sup>+</sup>] = pH / 10"],explain:"Rearranging pH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>[H<sup>+</sup>] gives [H<sup>+</sup>] = 10<sup>&minus;pH</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"What does a change of exactly one pH unit represent in terms of [H<sup>+</sup>]?",choices:["A 10-fold change in [H<sup>+</sup>]","A 2-fold change in [H<sup>+</sup>]","A 100-fold change in [H<sup>+</sup>]","No change in [H<sup>+</sup>]"],explain:"Because the pH scale is logarithmic (base 10), a change of one pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change in [H<sup>+</sup>]."},
{ch:19,q:"How many decimal places should a calculated pH value normally be given to?",choices:["The number of decimal places should equal the number of significant figures in the original concentration","Always exactly two decimal places, regardless of the data","Always zero decimal places","The number of decimal places should equal the pH value itself"],explain:"Since the digits to the left of the decimal point come from the exponential part of the number and are not significant, the number of decimal places in a calculated pH should match the significant figures in the original concentration."},
{ch:19,q:"How is pOH defined?",choices:["pOH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","pOH = log<sub>10</sub>[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","pOH = 14 &minus; [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","pOH = [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] &times; 14"],explain:"pOH is defined analogously to pH, as the negative base-10 logarithm of [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]: pOH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]."},
{ch:19,q:"What is the relationship between pH and pOH at 298 K?",choices:["pH + pOH = 14.00","pH &minus; pOH = 14.00","pH &times; pOH = 14.00","pH = pOH always"],explain:"Taking negative logarithms of K<sub>w</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] gives pH + pOH = pK<sub>w</sub> = 14.00 at 298 K."},
{ch:19,q:"Lemon juice has a pH of 2.90 at 298 K. What is [H<sup>+</sup>]?",choices:["1.26 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","2.90 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","7.94 &times; 10<sup>&minus;12</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[H<sup>+</sup>] = 10<sup>&minus;pH</sup> = 10<sup>&minus;2.90</sup> = 1.26 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"Lemon juice has a pH of 2.90 at 298 K. What is its pOH?",choices:["11.10","2.90","14.00","8.20"],explain:"pOH = 14.00 &minus; pH = 14.00 &minus; 2.90 = 11.10."},
{ch:19,q:"Lemon juice has a pH of 2.90 at 298 K. What is [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]?",choices:["7.94 &times; 10<sup>&minus;12</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.26 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.10 &times; 10<sup>&minus;11</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","2.90 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = 10<sup>&minus;pOH</sup> = 10<sup>&minus;11.10</sup> = 7.94 &times; 10<sup>&minus;12</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> (check: 1.26 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> &times; 7.94 &times; 10<sup>&minus;12</sup> = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> = K<sub>w</sub>, consistent)."},
{ch:19,q:"How is K<sub>w</sub> defined?",choices:["K<sub>w</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","K<sub>w</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>] / [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","K<sub>w</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>] + [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","K<sub>w</sub> = [H<sub>2</sub>O]"],explain:"K<sub>w</sub>, the ion product constant of water, is K<sub>w</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]; it equals 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> at 298 K."},
{ch:19,q:"In pure water at 298 K, what are [H<sup>+</sup>] and pH?",choices:["[H<sup>+</sup>] = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, pH = 7.00","[H<sup>+</sup>] = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, pH = 14.00","[H<sup>+</sup>] = 1.00 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, pH = 0.00","[H<sup>+</sup>] = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, pH = 0.00"],explain:"In pure water, [H<sup>+</sup>] = [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = &radic;K<sub>w</sub> = &radic;(1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup>) = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, giving pH = 7.00 at 298 K."},
{ch:19,q:"At 298 K, how is an acidic solution classified in terms of [H<sup>+</sup>] and [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]?",choices:["[H<sup>+</sup>] &gt; [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], pH &lt; 7","[H<sup>+</sup>] &lt; [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], pH &gt; 7","[H<sup>+</sup>] = [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], pH = 7","[H<sup>+</sup>] = 0, pH = 14"],explain:"An acidic solution at 298 K has [H<sup>+</sup>] &gt; [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], giving pH &lt; 7."},
{ch:19,q:"Why does increasing temperature decrease the pH of pure water, even though pure water remains neutral?",choices:["The dissociation of water is endothermic, so heating shifts the equilibrium right, increasing K<sub>w</sub> and [H<sup>+</sup>], while [H<sup>+</sup>] and [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] remain equal to each other","The dissociation of water is exothermic, so heating shifts the equilibrium left","Pure water becomes acidic (not neutral) when heated","K<sub>w</sub> is independent of temperature"],explain:"Water's dissociation is endothermic, so increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium right, increasing K<sub>w</sub> and therefore [H<sup>+</sup>] (lowering pH); since [H<sup>+</sup>] still equals [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], the water remains neutral even though pH 7.00 only applies at 298 K."},
{ch:19,q:"Why should temperature always be stated alongside a pH measurement?",choices:["Because K<sub>w</sub> is temperature-dependent, so the pH corresponding to a neutral solution (pH 7.00) is only valid at 298 K","Because pH meters only function at one fixed temperature","Because pH has no meaning without a stated temperature at all","Because temperature changes always make a solution acidic"],explain:"Since K<sub>w</sub> changes with temperature, the pH value corresponding to neutrality (where [H<sup>+</sup>] = [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]) also changes with temperature; pH 7.00 only means neutral at 298 K specifically."},
{ch:19,q:"What distinguishes a strong acid from a weak acid?",choices:["A strong acid dissociates almost fully; a weak acid dissociates only partially, with its equilibrium well to the left","A strong acid is always more concentrated than a weak acid","A strong acid always has a higher molar mass than a weak acid","A strong acid cannot exist in dilute solution"],explain:"Strong acids dissociate essentially completely, with the equilibrium lying far to the right; weak acids dissociate only partially, with the equilibrium lying well to the left."},
{ch:19,q:"Why must strong/weak never be confused with concentrated/dilute?",choices:["Strong/weak describes the inherent extent of dissociation, while concentrated/dilute describes moles of solute per unit volume &ndash; a strong acid can be dilute, and a weak acid can be concentrated","They are two names for the same property","A strong acid is always concentrated and a weak acid is always dilute","Dilution always converts a strong acid into a weak acid"],explain:"Strong/weak is an inherent property related to the extent of dissociation, independent of concentrated/dilute, which refers to the amount of solute dissolved per unit volume; a strong acid can be dilute and a weak acid can be concentrated."},
{ch:19,q:"Which of the following is a common strong acid?",choices:["HNO<sub>3</sub>","CH<sub>3</sub>COOH","HF","HCN"],explain:"HNO<sub>3</sub> (along with HCl, HBr, HI and H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) is one of the common strong acids; the others listed are weak acids."},
{ch:19,q:"Which of the following is a common weak base?",choices:["NH<sub>3</sub>","NaOH","KOH","LiOH"],explain:"NH<sub>3</sub> is a common weak base; NaOH, KOH and LiOH are all strong group 1 hydroxide bases."},
{ch:19,q:"At the same molar concentration, how does a strong acid compare with a weak acid in electrical conductivity?",choices:["The strong acid conducts more strongly, since it has a higher concentration of ions","The weak acid conducts more strongly","Both conduct identically, since concentration is the same","Neither acid conducts electricity at all"],explain:"At equal molar concentration, a strong acid dissociates more fully, producing a higher concentration of ions and so conducting electricity more strongly than a weak acid."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the K<sub>a</sub> expression for a generic weak acid HA dissociating as HA(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq) + A<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)?",choices:["K<sub>a</sub> = ([H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>&minus;</sup>]) / [HA]","K<sub>a</sub> = [HA] / ([H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>&minus;</sup>])","K<sub>a</sub> = [HA][H<sub>2</sub>O] / ([H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>&minus;</sup>])","K<sub>a</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>] + [A<sup>&minus;</sup>] &minus; [HA]"],explain:"Since [H<sub>2</sub>O] is essentially constant, it is absorbed into the modified equilibrium constant K<sub>a</sub> = ([H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>&minus;</sup>]) / [HA]."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the K<sub>b</sub> expression for a generic weak base B ionizing as B(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; BH<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)?",choices:["K<sub>b</sub> = ([BH<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]) / [B]","K<sub>b</sub> = [B] / ([BH<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>])","K<sub>b</sub> = [B][H<sub>2</sub>O] / ([BH<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>])","K<sub>b</sub> = [BH<sup>+</sup>] &minus; [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]"],explain:"K<sub>b</sub> = ([BH<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]) / [B], with [H<sub>2</sub>O] absorbed into the constant since it is essentially unchanged."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Write the K<sub>a</sub> expression for CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq).",choices:["K<sub>a</sub> = ([CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>][H<sup>+</sup>]) / [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]","K<sub>a</sub> = [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH] / ([CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>][H<sup>+</sup>])","K<sub>a</sub> = [CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>] + [H<sup>+</sup>]","K<sub>a</sub> = [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH][H<sup>+</sup>] / [CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>]"],explain:"Products over reactant: K<sub>a</sub> = ([CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>][H<sup>+</sup>]) / [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Write the K<sub>b</sub> expression for NH<sub>3</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq).",choices:["K<sub>b</sub> = ([NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]) / [NH<sub>3</sub>]","K<sub>b</sub> = [NH<sub>3</sub>] / ([NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>])","K<sub>b</sub> = [NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>] + [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","K<sub>b</sub> = [NH<sub>3</sub>][NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>] / [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]"],explain:"K<sub>b</sub> = ([NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]) / [NH<sub>3</sub>]."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What does a higher K<sub>a</sub> value indicate about an acid?",choices:["A greater extent of dissociation and therefore a stronger acid","A smaller extent of dissociation and therefore a weaker acid","Nothing about acid strength, only about reaction rate","That the acid is a strong acid in the Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry sense, dissociating completely"],explain:"K<sub>a</sub> is an equilibrium constant: the higher its value, the greater the extent of dissociation and the stronger the acid."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why are K<sub>a</sub> and K<sub>b</sub> not normally quoted for strong acids and bases?",choices:["Because strong acids/bases dissociate essentially completely, so the equilibrium constant would be extremely large and the concept is most useful for weak acids/bases","Because strong acids and bases have no equilibrium at all","Because K<sub>a</sub> and K<sub>b</sub> only apply to gaseous reactions","Because strong acids and bases always have K<sub>a</sub> = K<sub>b</sub> = 1"],explain:"Since strong acids/bases dissociate essentially completely, K<sub>a</sub>/K<sub>b</sub> would be enormous and not experimentally useful; the concept is most informative for weak acids/bases where a genuine equilibrium mixture exists."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A 0.0100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> solution of ethanoic acid has a pH of 3.4 at 298 K. What is [H<sup>+</sup>] at equilibrium?",choices:["4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","3.4 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3.4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[H<sup>+</sup>] = 10<sup>&minus;pH</sup> = 10<sup>&minus;3.4</sup> = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: For the same 0.0100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> ethanoic acid solution (pH 3.4, [H<sup>+</sup>] = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), what approximation is made for [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub>, and why?",choices:["[CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; 0.0100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, since the small amount dissociated is negligible compared with the initial concentration","[CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, equal to [H<sup>+</sup>]","[CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub> = 0 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, since it has fully dissociated","[CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub> cannot be estimated without further data"],explain:"Since only a tiny fraction (4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> out of 0.0100) has dissociated, [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; 0.0100 &minus; 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> &asymp; 0.0100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Using [H<sup>+</sup>] = [CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>] = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> and [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; 0.0100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, calculate K<sub>a</sub>.",choices:["1.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>","4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>","1.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup>","4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>a</sub> = (4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>)<sup>2</sup> / 0.0100 = 1.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> / 0.0100 = 1.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: How is pK<sub>a</sub> related to K<sub>a</sub>?",choices:["pK<sub>a</sub> = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>K<sub>a</sub>","pK<sub>a</sub> = log<sub>10</sub>K<sub>a</sub>","pK<sub>a</sub> = 10<sup>K<sub>a</sub></sup>","pK<sub>a</sub> = 1 / K<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"pK<sub>a</sub> is defined exactly as pH is from [H<sup>+</sup>]: pK<sub>a</sub> = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>K<sub>a</sub>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A smaller pK<sub>a</sub> value corresponds to:",choices:["A larger K<sub>a</sub> and so a stronger acid","A smaller K<sub>a</sub> and so a weaker acid","No change in acid strength","A strong base, never an acid"],explain:"Because pK<sub>a</sub> is inversely related to K<sub>a</sub>, a smaller pK<sub>a</sub> means a larger K<sub>a</sub>, corresponding to a stronger (more fully dissociated) acid."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A change of one unit in pK<sub>a</sub> represents what change in K<sub>a</sub>?",choices:["A tenfold change","A twofold change","A hundredfold change","No change at all"],explain:"Since pK<sub>a</sub> is a base-10 logarithmic scale, a change of one pK<sub>a</sub> unit represents a tenfold change in K<sub>a</sub>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the relationship between K<sub>a</sub>, K<sub>b</sub> and K<sub>w</sub> for a conjugate acid&ndash;base pair?",choices:["K<sub>a</sub> &times; K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub>","K<sub>a</sub> + K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub>","K<sub>a</sub> &minus; K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub>","K<sub>a</sub> / K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub>"],explain:"Multiplying the K<sub>a</sub> expression for HA by the K<sub>b</sub> expression for its conjugate base A<sup>&minus;</sup> causes [HA] and [A<sup>&minus;</sup>] to cancel, leaving K<sub>a</sub> &times; K<sub>b</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = K<sub>w</sub>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the equivalent logarithmic form of K<sub>a</sub> &times; K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub> at 298 K?",choices:["pK<sub>a</sub> + pK<sub>b</sub> = 14.00","pK<sub>a</sub> &times; pK<sub>b</sub> = 14.00","pK<sub>a</sub> &minus; pK<sub>b</sub> = 14.00","pK<sub>a</sub> / pK<sub>b</sub> = 14.00"],explain:"Taking negative logarithms of K<sub>a</sub> &times; K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub> gives pK<sub>a</sub> + pK<sub>b</sub> = pK<sub>w</sub> = 14.00 at 298 K."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: K<sub>a</sub> of ethanoic acid is 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> at 298 K. What is pK<sub>a</sub>?",choices:["4.74","5.74","1.8","9.26"],explain:"pK<sub>a</sub> = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) = 4.74."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Given K<sub>a</sub>(CH<sub>3</sub>COOH) = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> at 298 K, calculate K<sub>b</sub> of its conjugate base, CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>.",choices:["5.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;10</sup>","1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>","1.8 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>","5.6 &times; 10<sup>10</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub> / K<sub>a</sub> = (1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup>) / (1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) = 5.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;10</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Given K<sub>b</sub>(CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>) = 5.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;10</sup>, what is pK<sub>b</sub>, and does it satisfy pK<sub>a</sub> + pK<sub>b</sub> = 14.00 with pK<sub>a</sub> = 4.74?",choices:["pK<sub>b</sub> = 9.26, and 4.74 + 9.26 = 14.00, confirming the relationship","pK<sub>b</sub> = 10.00, which does not satisfy the relationship","pK<sub>b</sub> = 4.74, identical to pK<sub>a</sub>","pK<sub>b</sub> = 0.56, which does not satisfy the relationship"],explain:"pK<sub>b</sub> = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(5.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;10</sup>) = 9.26, and 4.74 + 9.26 = 14.00, confirming K<sub>a</sub> &times; K<sub>b</sub> = K<sub>w</sub>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the pH of a solution formed from a salt of a weak acid and a strong base (e.g. sodium ethanoate), and why?",choices:["pH &gt; 7, because the anion hydrolyses, releasing OH<sup>&minus;</sup>: CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)","pH &lt; 7, because the anion releases H<sup>+</sup>","pH = 7, since all salts are neutral","pH &gt; 7, because the cation hydrolyses"],explain:"The anion of a weak acid is itself a weak base and hydrolyses, releasing OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, giving a solution with pH &gt; 7."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the pH of a solution formed from a salt of a strong acid and a weak base (e.g. ammonium chloride), and why?",choices:["pH &lt; 7, because the cation hydrolyses, releasing H<sup>+</sup>: NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; NH<sub>3</sub>(aq) + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>(aq)","pH &gt; 7, because the cation releases OH<sup>&minus;</sup>","pH = 7, since all ammonium salts are neutral","pH &lt; 7, because the anion hydrolyses"],explain:"The cation of a weak base is itself a weak acid and hydrolyses, releasing H<sup>+</sup>, giving a solution with pH &lt; 7."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: In the weak-acid ICE table with initial concentration C and dissociated amount x, what is the full (unapproximated) K<sub>a</sub> expression?",choices:["K<sub>a</sub> = x<sup>2</sup> / (C &minus; x)","K<sub>a</sub> = x<sup>2</sup> / C","K<sub>a</sub> = (C &minus; x) / x<sup>2</sup>","K<sub>a</sub> = x / (C &minus; x)"],explain:"At equilibrium [HA] = C &minus; x and [H<sup>+</sup>] = [A<sup>&minus;</sup>] = x, so K<sub>a</sub> = x<sup>2</sup> / (C &minus; x)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What simplified formula is used to find [H<sup>+</sup>] for a weak acid, assuming the degree of dissociation is small?",choices:["[H<sup>+</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>a</sub> &times; C)","[H<sup>+</sup>] = K<sub>a</sub> / C","[H<sup>+</sup>] = K<sub>a</sub> &times; C","[H<sup>+</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>a</sub> / C)"],explain:"Approximating C &minus; x &asymp; C gives K<sub>a</sub> &asymp; x<sup>2</sup>/C, so [H<sup>+</sup>] = x &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>a</sub> &times; C)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What rule of thumb is used to check the validity of the approximation [H<sup>+</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>a</sub>C)?",choices:["The calculated [H<sup>+</sup>] should be less than about 5% of the initial concentration C","The calculated [H<sup>+</sup>] should be greater than 50% of C","K<sub>a</sub> must be greater than 1","C must be less than 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"The approximation is valid when the degree of dissociation is small, which can be checked by confirming the calculated [H<sup>+</sup>] is less than about 5% of C (equivalently, C/K<sub>a</sub> is large, typically &gt; 400&ndash;500)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: If the 5% validity check fails for a weak acid pH calculation, what must be done instead?",choices:["The full quadratic K<sub>a</sub> = x<sup>2</sup>/(C &minus; x) must be solved","The calculation should be abandoned entirely","K<sub>a</sub> should simply be doubled to compensate","The square-root approximation should still be used, since it is always close enough"],explain:"If the approximation fails the 5% check, the full quadratic K<sub>a</sub> = x<sup>2</sup>/(C &minus; x) must be solved instead, as in the general equilibrium treatment of Reactivity 2.3."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A 0.75 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> solution of ethanoic acid has K<sub>a</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> at 298 K. Calculate [H<sup>+</sup>].",choices:["3.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.35 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","7.5 &times; 10<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[H<sup>+</sup>] = &radic;(K<sub>a</sub> &times; C) = &radic;(1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> &times; 0.75) = &radic;(1.35 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) = 3.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: For the 0.75 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> ethanoic acid example ([H<sup>+</sup>] = 3.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), what is the pH, and is the 5% approximation valid?",choices:["pH = 2.43; valid, since 3.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> is only 0.49% of 0.75","pH = 2.43; not valid, since the dissociation is too large","pH = 4.74; valid","pH = 0.12; valid, since this is a strong acid"],explain:"pH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(3.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>) = 2.43. Since 3.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> is only 0.49% of 0.75, well under 5%, the approximation is confirmed valid."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why is 0.75 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> ethanoic acid (pH 2.43) far less acidic than 0.75 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> HCl (pH 0.12) at the same concentration?",choices:["Only a small fraction of the ethanoic acid has dissociated, since it is weak, whereas HCl is assumed to dissociate fully as a strong acid","Ethanoic acid and HCl should give identical pH values at equal concentration","HCl has a lower molar mass, making it less acidic","Ethanoic acid reacts with water to produce OH<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"HCl, a strong acid, is assumed to dissociate fully, so [H<sup>+</sup>] = C = 0.75 directly; ethanoic acid, a weak acid, dissociates only slightly, giving a much lower [H<sup>+</sup>] and therefore a higher (less acidic) pH at the same concentration."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What simplified formula is used to find [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] for a weak base, assuming the degree of ionization is small?",choices:["[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>b</sub> &times; C)","[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = K<sub>b</sub> / C","[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = K<sub>b</sub> &times; C","[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>b</sub> / C)"],explain:"By the same reasoning as for a weak acid, K<sub>b</sub> &asymp; x<sup>2</sup>/C, so [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = x &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>b</sub> &times; C)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A 0.20 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> solution of ammonia has K<sub>b</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> at 298 K. Calculate [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>].",choices:["1.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","3.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;6</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = &radic;(K<sub>b</sub> &times; C) = &radic;(1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> &times; 0.20) = &radic;(3.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;6</sup>) = 1.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: For the 0.20 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> ammonia example ([OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = 1.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), what is pOH, and what is pH at 298 K?",choices:["pOH = 2.72, pH = 11.28","pOH = 2.72, pH = 2.72","pOH = 11.28, pH = 2.72","pOH = 1.9, pH = 12.1"],explain:"pOH = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(1.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>) = 2.72; pH = 14.00 &minus; pOH = 14.00 &minus; 2.72 = 11.28."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is [H<sup>+</sup>] for a strong acid of concentration C, assuming full dissociation?",choices:["[H<sup>+</sup>] = C","[H<sup>+</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>a</sub>C)","[H<sup>+</sup>] = C / 2","[H<sup>+</sup>] = &radic;C"],explain:"A strong acid is assumed to dissociate fully, so [H<sup>+</sup>] equals the initial concentration C directly (adjusted for any stoichiometric factor if more than one acidic proton is released per formula unit)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which pH/pOH calculation route is correct for a weak base of known concentration C and K<sub>b</sub>?",choices:["[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>b</sub>C)","[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = C","[H<sup>+</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>b</sub>C)","[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] = K<sub>b</sub> &times; C directly, with no square root"],explain:"For a weak base, [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] &asymp; &radic;(K<sub>b</sub>C), by analogy with the weak acid case, provided the degree of ionization is small."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Using the table of weak acids (HF, K<sub>a</sub> = 6.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>; HCOOH, K<sub>a</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH, K<sub>a</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>), which is the strongest acid?",choices:["HF, since it has the largest K<sub>a</sub>","CH<sub>3</sub>COOH, since it has the smallest K<sub>a</sub>","HCOOH, since it is intermediate","All three are equally strong"],explain:"The acid with the largest K<sub>a</sub> is the most fully dissociated and therefore the strongest; HF (6.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>) is the strongest of the three listed."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Using pK<sub>a</sub> values (HF = 3.17, HCN = 9.21), which is the stronger acid, and what does this imply about the relative strength of their conjugate bases, F<sup>&minus;</sup> and CN<sup>&minus;</sup>?",choices:["HF is the stronger acid (smaller pK<sub>a</sub>); F<sup>&minus;</sup> is therefore the weaker conjugate base, and CN<sup>&minus;</sup> the stronger one","HCN is the stronger acid; CN<sup>&minus;</sup> is the weaker conjugate base","Both acids are equally strong, since both pK<sub>a</sub> values are positive","HF is the stronger acid; F<sup>&minus;</sup> is therefore also the stronger conjugate base"],explain:"HF has the smaller pK<sub>a</sub> and so is the stronger acid; since acid strength and conjugate base strength are inversely related, F<sup>&minus;</sup> (conjugate base of the stronger acid) is the weaker base, while CN<sup>&minus;</sup> is the stronger base."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why does lengthening the hydrocarbon chain of a carboxylic acid (ethanoic &rarr; propanoic) slightly weaken the acid?",choices:["Ethanoic acid has K<sub>a</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>, larger than propanoic acid's K<sub>a</sub> = 1.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>, so ethanoic acid is the stronger acid of the two","Propanoic acid has the larger K<sub>a</sub> of the two, making it the stronger acid","Chain length has no effect on K<sub>a</sub> at all","Both acids have identical K<sub>a</sub> values"],explain:"According to the data table, ethanoic acid (K<sub>a</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) is slightly stronger than propanoic acid (K<sub>a</sub> = 1.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>), showing that lengthening the chain slightly weakens the acid."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Using the K<sub>b</sub> table (NH<sub>3</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>, CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> = 4.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>), why is methylamine a stronger base than ammonia?",choices:["Methylamine has a larger K<sub>b</sub>, because the electron-donating alkyl group increases the availability of nitrogen's lone pair for accepting H<sup>+</sup>","Methylamine has a smaller K<sub>b</sub> than ammonia","Methylamine contains no nitrogen lone pair at all","Ammonia is actually the stronger base of the two"],explain:"Methylamine's larger K<sub>b</sub> (4.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> vs 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) reflects that its electron-donating methyl group increases the availability of the nitrogen lone pair for accepting a proton, making it a stronger base than ammonia."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is a buffer solution?",choices:["A solution that resists change in pH on the addition of small amounts of acid or base","A solution whose pH is always exactly 7","A solution made only from a strong acid and a strong base","A solution with no dissolved ions at all"],explain:"A buffer solution resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added to it."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which combination of components can form an acidic buffer?",choices:["A weak acid together with a soluble salt of its conjugate base","A strong acid together with a soluble salt of its conjugate base","A weak acid together with a strong acid","Pure water alone"],explain:"An acidic buffer is made from a weak acid and a soluble salt of its conjugate base, e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) and NaCH<sub>3</sub>COO(aq)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which combination of components can form a basic buffer?",choices:["A weak base together with a soluble salt of its conjugate acid","A strong base together with a soluble salt of its conjugate acid","A weak base together with a strong base","Distilled water alone"],explain:"A basic buffer is made from a weak base and a soluble salt of its conjugate acid, e.g. NH<sub>3</sub>(aq) and NH<sub>4</sub>Cl(aq)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: In an acidic buffer made of HA and its salt MA, how is added acid (H<sup>+</sup>) removed?",choices:["By the conjugate base: A<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; HA(aq)","By the weak acid: HA(aq) + H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>A<sup>+</sup>(aq)","Added acid cannot be removed by a buffer at all","By water alone, independent of the buffer components"],explain:"The conjugate base reservoir (A<sup>&minus;</sup>) reacts with added H<sup>+</sup> to re-form HA, removing the added acid and resisting a pH drop."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: In the same acidic buffer, how is added base (OH<sup>&minus;</sup>) removed?",choices:["By the weak acid: HA(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; A<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","By the conjugate base A<sup>&minus;</sup>, which reacts directly with OH<sup>&minus;</sup>","Added base cannot be removed by a buffer at all","Only by evaporation of water"],explain:"The weak acid reservoir (HA) reacts with added OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, neutralizing it and forming A<sup>&minus;</sup> and water, resisting a pH rise."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why must a buffer be made from a weak, rather than strong, conjugate acid&ndash;base pair?",choices:["A buffer must contain appreciable concentrations of both the acid and base forms at equilibrium, but a strong acid or base ionizes almost completely, leaving almost none of the undissociated form to react with added acid or base","Strong acids and bases are always too concentrated to be used in a buffer","A weak acid always has a pH closer to 7 than a strong acid","Buffers can only be made from gases, not from acids or bases in solution"],explain:"Because both the acid and base forms must be present in appreciable concentration to respond to added H<sup>+</sup> or OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, only a weak conjugate acid&ndash;base pair (existing in equilibrium) can supply both reservoirs simultaneously."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: 0.20 mol NH<sub>3</sub> and 0.10 mol HCl are dissolved in 1.00 dm<sup>3</sup> of water. Does this mixture form a buffer?",choices:["Yes: the HCl reacts with NH<sub>3</sub> to form 0.10 mol NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>, leaving 0.10 mol NH<sub>3</sub> unreacted, giving a conjugate NH<sub>3</sub>/NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> pair","No, because HCl is a strong acid and cannot be part of a buffer system","No, because this mixture contains no conjugate acid&ndash;base pair","Yes, but only because NH<sub>3</sub> is present in excess of 1.00 mol"],explain:"The strong acid HCl reacts completely with some of the NH<sub>3</sub>, converting 0.10 mol to NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> and leaving 0.10 mol NH<sub>3</sub> unreacted; the resulting NH<sub>3</sub>/NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> conjugate pair forms a buffer."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: 0.20 mol CH<sub>3</sub>COOH and 0.20 mol HCl are dissolved in 1.00 dm<sup>3</sup> of water. Does this mixture form a buffer?",choices:["No, because this mixture contains two different acids and no conjugate base reservoir","Yes, because both are acids of similar strength","Yes, because CH<sub>3</sub>COOH is a weak acid","No, because HCl always destroys any buffering capacity present"],explain:"A mixture of two different acids with no conjugate base reservoir present is not a buffer; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH and HCl do not form a conjugate acid&ndash;base pair with each other."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why does diluting a buffer solution not change its pH?",choices:["K<sub>a</sub> (or K<sub>b</sub>) is unaffected by concentration, and dilution changes [acid] and [salt] by the same factor, leaving their ratio (and hence the pH) unchanged","Dilution always increases the pH of any solution","Dilution always decreases the pH of any solution","Buffers have no defined pH, so dilution is irrelevant"],explain:"Since dilution scales both [acid] and [salt] (or [base] and [salt]) by the same factor, their ratio is unchanged; because pH depends only on pK<sub>a</sub> and this ratio (Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch), diluting a buffer leaves its pH unchanged."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What does dilution reduce in a buffer solution, even though the pH itself is unchanged?",choices:["Its buffering capacity &ndash; the absolute amount of acid or base it can neutralize before the pH changes significantly","Its pK<sub>a</sub> value","The identity of its conjugate acid&ndash;base pair","Its ability to resist pH change at all, making it behave as an unbuffered solution"],explain:"Dilution reduces the actual concentrations (and so the absolute amounts) of the buffer components present, reducing the buffering capacity even though the pH ratio, and hence the pH itself, is unchanged."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer?",choices:["pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid])","pH = pK<sub>a</sub> &minus; log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid])","pH = pK<sub>a</sub> &times; log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid])","pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([acid]/[salt])"],explain:"Taking negative logarithms of [H<sup>+</sup>] = K<sub>a</sub> &times; ([acid]/[salt]) gives pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid])."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What two approximations underlie the derivation of the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation?",choices:["The weak acid's dissociation is negligible, so [HA]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; [HA]<sub>initial</sub>; and the salt is fully dissociated, so [A<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; [salt]<sub>initial</sub>","Both the acid and the salt are assumed to dissociate fully","Neither the acid nor the salt dissociates at all","The temperature of the buffer is assumed to be 0 K"],explain:"The equation assumes the weak acid barely dissociates ([HA]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; [HA]<sub>initial</sub>) and that the soluble salt dissociates fully ([A<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; [salt]<sub>initial</sub>)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the parallel Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation for a basic buffer of weak base B and salt BHX?",choices:["pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[base])","pH = pK<sub>b</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[base])","pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> &minus; log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[base])","pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([base]/[salt])"],explain:"For a basic buffer, pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[base]), with pH then found from pH = 14.00 &minus; pOH."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: When [salt] = [acid] in a buffer, what does the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation simplify to?",choices:["pH = pK<sub>a</sub> exactly, since log<sub>10</sub>(1) = 0","pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + 1","pH = 2pK<sub>a</sub>","pH = 0, since the ratio is 1"],explain:"When [salt] = [acid], the ratio is 1 and log<sub>10</sub>(1) = 0, so pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + 0 = pK<sub>a</sub> exactly."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Calculate the pH at 298 K of a buffer made from equal volumes of 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> CH<sub>3</sub>COOH and 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> NaCH<sub>3</sub>COO. K<sub>a</sub>(CH<sub>3</sub>COOH) = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>.",choices:["4.74","4.26","9.26","5.24"],explain:"pK<sub>a</sub> = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) = 4.74. Since equal volumes of equal concentrations give [salt] = [acid], pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>(1) = 4.74."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A buffer is prepared from 0.30 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> HCOOH (pK<sub>a</sub> = 3.75) and 0.15 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> HCOONa. Calculate its pH.",choices:["3.45","3.75","4.05","3.15"],explain:"pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid]) = 3.75 + log<sub>10</sub>(0.15/0.30) = 3.75 + log<sub>10</sub>(0.50) = 3.75 &minus; 0.30 = 3.45."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why is the pH of the HCOOH/HCOONa buffer above (3.45) lower than its pK<sub>a</sub> (3.75)?",choices:["Because [salt] &lt; [acid] in this buffer, making it slightly more acidic than an equimolar mixture of the same acid and salt","Because [salt] &gt; [acid] in this buffer","Because HCOOH is actually a strong acid","Because the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation always gives pH &lt; pK<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"Since [salt] (0.15) is less than [acid] (0.30), log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid]) is negative, lowering the pH below pK<sub>a</sub>, making the buffer slightly more acidic than an equimolar mixture."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: How should a weak acid be chosen to prepare a buffer at a specific target pH?",choices:["Choose a weak acid whose pK<sub>a</sub> is close to the target pH, then fine-tune the exact value using the salt-to-acid ratio in the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation","Choose any weak acid at all, since the ratio alone determines the final pH","Choose only a strong acid, since weak acids cannot reach every pH value","Choose a weak acid whose pK<sub>b</sub> equals the target pH"],explain:"The pK<sub>a</sub> of the chosen acid sets the pH the buffer is centred on; the salt-to-acid ratio is then adjusted using Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch to fine-tune the exact target pH."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What does the pH of a buffer solution depend on?",choices:["The pK<sub>a</sub> (or pK<sub>b</sub>) of its weak acid/base component, and the ratio of conjugate base to acid (or conjugate acid to base) present","Only the total volume of the solution","Only the identity of the counter-ion in the salt","Only the temperature, never on the components present"],explain:"The buffer's pH depends on exactly two things: the pK<sub>a</sub> (or pK<sub>b</sub>) of the weak acid or base, which sets the centre point, and the salt-to-acid (or salt-to-base) concentration ratio, which fine-tunes the pH around that centre."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: In the four titration curve cases described, where 50.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> acid is titrated with 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> base, at what volume of base added does the equivalence point occur?",choices:["50.0 cm<sup>3</sup>","25.0 cm<sup>3</sup>","100.0 cm<sup>3</sup>","0 cm<sup>3</sup>"],explain:"Since both solutions are 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> monoprotic, and the acid volume is 50.0 cm<sup>3</sup>, exactly 50.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of base is needed to reach the stoichiometric equivalence point."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the pH at the equivalence point of a strong acid&ndash;strong base titration, and why?",choices:["Exactly 7, because the salt formed contains ions from a strong acid and strong base, neither of which hydrolyses","Greater than 7, because the anion hydrolyses","Less than 7, because the cation hydrolyses","Cannot be determined without further data"],explain:"The salt formed (e.g. NaCl) contains ions from a strong acid and a strong base, so neither hydrolyses appreciably, giving pH = 7 exactly at equivalence."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why does a strong acid&ndash;strong base titration give the sharpest, largest jump in pH of the four cases?",choices:["Because the reaction goes essentially to completion with no competing weak equilibria, producing the most dramatic change in [H<sup>+</sup>] near equivalence","Because strong acid&ndash;strong base reactions never reach equivalence","Because the pH starts and ends at the same value","Because no indicator can be used for this titration"],explain:"With no weak acid or weak base equilibrium buffering the pH, the strong acid&ndash;strong base reaction shows the sharpest, largest jump in pH, from about pH 3 to pH 11, centred on the equivalence point."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the pH at the equivalence point of a weak acid&ndash;strong base titration, and why?",choices:["Greater than 7, because the anion of the weak acid hydrolyses, releasing OH<sup>&minus;</sup>","Exactly 7, since the acid and base fully neutralize each other","Less than 7, because the salt formed is strongly acidic","Cannot be determined, since weak acids never reach equivalence"],explain:"At equivalence, the flask contains only the salt of a weak acid and a strong base; the anion hydrolyses (e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>), giving pH &gt; 7."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: In a weak acid&ndash;strong base titration curve, what region extends through roughly the first half of the volume added, before the sharp jump at equivalence?",choices:["The buffer region, where the flask contains a mixture of the weak acid and its conjugate base salt","The region where pH stays exactly at 7 throughout","The region where only the strong base is present","A region of zero pH change identical to pure water"],explain:"As base is gradually added, a mixture of the remaining weak acid and the newly formed conjugate base salt builds up in the flask, acting as a buffer and keeping the pH changing only slowly over this region."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the half-equivalence point of a titration, and what does it tell us?",choices:["The point at which exactly half the original weak acid has been converted to its conjugate base salt, where [acid] = [salt] and so pH = pK<sub>a</sub>","The point at which exactly twice the equivalence volume of base has been added","The point at which the indicator permanently changes colour","The point where pH always equals exactly 7.00"],explain:"At half the equivalence volume, exactly half the original acid has reacted, giving [acid]<sub>eqm</sub> = [salt]<sub>eqm</sub>; by the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation, pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>(1) = pK<sub>a</sub> at this point."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: 25.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of 0.100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> CH<sub>3</sub>COOH is titrated with 0.100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> NaOH, with equivalence at 25.0 cm<sup>3</sup>. At 12.5 cm<sup>3</sup> of NaOH added, the measured pH is 4.74. What does this tell us?",choices:["12.5 cm<sup>3</sup> is the half-equivalence point, so pH = pK<sub>a</sub>, giving pK<sub>a</sub>(CH<sub>3</sub>COOH) = 4.74","12.5 cm<sup>3</sup> is the equivalence point, so pH = 7 should have been observed","The acid used must actually be a strong acid","pK<sub>b</sub> of the conjugate base equals 4.74 directly, with no further calculation needed"],explain:"12.5 cm<sup>3</sup> is exactly half of the 25.0 cm<sup>3</sup> needed for equivalence, so [CH<sub>3</sub>COOH] = [CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>&minus;</sup>] at this point, giving pH = pK<sub>a</sub> = 4.74, consistent with K<sub>a</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why is reading the pH at the half-equivalence point considered one of the most useful applications of a titration curve?",choices:["It allows the pK<sub>a</sub> of a weak acid to be determined experimentally, directly from the graph","It allows the exact concentration of the strong base to be found without any calculation","It shows that the titration curve is symmetrical about pH 7 in every case","It confirms that no indicator is needed for this titration"],explain:"Since pH = pK<sub>a</sub> exactly at the half-equivalence point, reading the pH off the curve at this volume gives an experimental value for the weak acid's pK<sub>a</sub>, without needing any further calculation."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the pH at the equivalence point of a strong acid&ndash;weak base titration, and why?",choices:["Less than 7, because the cation of the weak base hydrolyses, releasing H<sup>+</sup>","Greater than 7, because the cation releases OH<sup>&minus;</sup>","Exactly 7, since the acid and base are of equal strength","Cannot be determined, since weak bases never reach equivalence"],explain:"At equivalence, the salt formed contains the conjugate acid of the weak base; the cation hydrolyses (e.g. NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O &harr; NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>), giving pH &lt; 7."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: In a strong acid&ndash;weak base titration, what does the half-equivalence point allow to be determined, analogous to the weak acid case?",choices:["pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> of the weak base at that point","pH = pK<sub>a</sub> of the strong acid at that point","The exact K<sub>w</sub> value of water at that temperature","Nothing useful can be determined at this point"],explain:"By a parallel half-equivalence analysis, at half the equivalence volume of a strong acid&ndash;weak base titration, pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> of the weak base."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why is there no large, sharp jump in pH near the equivalence point of a weak acid&ndash;weak base titration?",choices:["Because both the acid and base are weak, several equilibria compete simultaneously, giving a gentle, almost featureless change in slope rather than a sharp jump","Because weak acids and weak bases never actually react with each other","Because the equivalence point does not exist for this combination","Because this titration always has pH = 7 throughout, with no change at all"],explain:"With both reactants weak, multiple competing equilibria prevent any single, dramatic jump in pH; the curve shows only a gentle, smooth change in slope through the middle, with no sharp feature marking equivalence."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why can the pH at equivalence not be simply predicted for a weak acid&ndash;weak base titration?",choices:["It depends on the relative K<sub>a</sub> and K<sub>b</sub> values of the two reactants, with no single general rule","It is always exactly 7, just like the strong&ndash;strong case","It is always greater than 7, just like the weak acid&ndash;strong base case","It is always less than 7, just like the strong acid&ndash;weak base case"],explain:"Because both the acid and base are weak, the pH at equivalence depends on the relative strengths (K<sub>a</sub> and K<sub>b</sub> values) of the two reactants, so no single rule like &gt;7, &lt;7, or =7 can be generalized."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why is a pH curve (or an indicator) not a reliable way to locate the equivalence point of a weak acid&ndash;weak base titration?",choices:["There is no sharp, near-vertical jump in pH to clearly mark the equivalence point","The pH curve for this combination always matches the strong acid&ndash;strong base case exactly","Weak acid&ndash;weak base reactions do not produce any measurable pH change at all","Indicators cannot be dissolved in weak acid&ndash;weak base mixtures"],explain:"Because there is no large, sharp jump in pH anywhere near equivalence for this combination, neither a pH curve nor an indicator can reliably pinpoint the equivalence point; other techniques such as conductimetric titration are used instead."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Rank the sharpness of the pH jump at equivalence for the four titration combinations, from sharpest to least sharp.",choices:["Strong&ndash;strong &gt; (weak acid&ndash;strong base and strong acid&ndash;weak base, both smaller and roughly comparable) &gt; weak&ndash;weak (no significant jump)","Weak&ndash;weak &gt; strong&ndash;strong &gt; weak acid&ndash;strong base &gt; strong acid&ndash;weak base","All four combinations give an equally sharp jump at equivalence","Strong acid&ndash;weak base gives the sharpest jump of all four"],explain:"Strong acid&ndash;strong base gives by far the largest, sharpest jump; weak acid&ndash;strong base and strong acid&ndash;weak base both give smaller jumps; weak acid&ndash;weak base gives no significant jump at all."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: An acid&ndash;base indicator, HInd, is itself best described as:",choices:["A weak acid (or weak base) whose undissociated and dissociated forms have distinctly different colours","A strong acid that dissociates completely regardless of pH","A catalyst that speeds up the main titration reaction","A substance with no acid&ndash;base properties of its own"],explain:"An indicator is a weak acid (or weak base) HInd &harr; H<sup>+</sup> + Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>, in which the undissociated form (HInd) and dissociated form (Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>) have distinctly different colours."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: By Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle, what happens to the HInd &harr; H<sup>+</sup> + Ind<sup>&minus;</sup> equilibrium when [H<sup>+</sup>] increases?",choices:["The equilibrium shifts left, favouring the colour of HInd","The equilibrium shifts right, favouring the colour of Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>","The equilibrium is unaffected by changes in [H<sup>+</sup>]","The indicator decomposes completely"],explain:"Increasing [H<sup>+</sup>] shifts the equilibrium left (by Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle), favouring the undissociated form HInd and its associated colour."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: At the midpoint of an indicator's colour change, what is true of [HInd] and [Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>], and what does this mean for pH and pK<sub>a</sub>?",choices:["[HInd] = [Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>], so K<sub>a</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>] and therefore pH = pK<sub>a</sub> of the indicator at this point","[HInd] = 0, so pH is undefined at this point","[Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>] = 0, so pH = 0 at this point","[HInd] and [Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>] are unrelated to pH or pK<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"At the exact midpoint, [HInd] = [Ind<sup>&minus;</sup>], so these terms cancel in the K<sub>a</sub> expression, leaving K<sub>a</sub> = [H<sup>+</sup>], i.e. pH = pK<sub>a</sub> of the indicator at its colour-change midpoint (its end point)."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Why does an indicator show an observable colour change over an end point range of roughly &plusmn;1 pH unit, rather than at one single, sharp pH value?",choices:["The human eye can distinguish the pure colour of one form from a mixture only once the ratio of concentrations reaches about 10:1","Indicators always dissociate in exactly two separate, unrelated steps","The eye can detect a colour change only at a precise ratio of exactly 1:1","Indicators have no fixed pK<sub>a</sub> value at all"],explain:"Because the eye can only distinguish the pure colour of one form once the concentration ratio reaches about 10:1 in either direction, the observable colour change spans roughly &plusmn;1 pH unit around the indicator's pK<sub>a</sub>, giving an end point range."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Methyl orange has pK<sub>a</sub> = 3.7 and an end point range of 3.1&ndash;4.4. Why is it unsuitable for titrating ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide?",choices:["Its end point range lies in the gently sloping buffer region of this weak acid&ndash;strong base curve, well before the equivalence point (around pH 8&ndash;9), so it would change colour too early","Its end point range exactly matches the equivalence point of this titration","It cannot dissolve in aqueous solution","It only works for titrations involving a weak base, never a weak acid"],explain:"Since the equivalence point of the ethanoic acid&ndash;NaOH titration lies around pH 8&ndash;9, methyl orange's end point range (3.1&ndash;4.4) falls in the gently sloping buffer region well before equivalence, causing it to change colour far too early to mark the correct end point."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which indicator is suitable for the titration of ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide, and why?",choices:["Phenolphthalein, since its end point range (8.2&ndash;10.0) coincides with the sharp part of the curve near the equivalence point (around pH 8&ndash;9)","Methyl orange, since it is the most commonly used indicator","Bromophenol blue, since its colour change is yellow to blue","Any indicator at all, since the choice of indicator never matters for a weak acid&ndash;strong base titration"],explain:"Phenolphthalein's end point range (8.2&ndash;10.0) matches the sharp, near-vertical part of the weak acid&ndash;strong base curve around the equivalence point (pH &gt; 7), making it the correct choice; methyl orange would change colour too early."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which indicator(s) are suitable for a strong acid&ndash;weak base titration, where the equivalence point lies in the range pH 3&ndash;7?",choices:["Methyl orange or bromophenol blue","Phenolphthalein only","Phenol red only","No indicator is suitable for this combination"],explain:"Since the equivalence point for a strong acid&ndash;weak base titration lies in the lower pH range (3&ndash;7), methyl orange or bromophenol blue (lower pK<sub>a</sub> indicators) are suitable; phenolphthalein would change colour too late."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What three steps are involved in choosing a suitable indicator for a given titration?",choices:["Identify the strong/weak nature of the acid and base; deduce the approximate pH at equivalence; select an indicator whose end point range matches that pH","Measure the exact volume of titrant added at the start of the experiment only","Choose the indicator with the brightest colour change, regardless of pH","Always choose phenolphthalein, since it works for every titration type"],explain:"Choosing an indicator requires: (1) identifying whether the acid and base are strong or weak, (2) deducing the approximate pH at equivalence from this, and (3) selecting an indicator whose end point range matches that pH."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: What is the key difference between the equivalence point and the end point of a titration?",choices:["The equivalence point is the exact stoichiometric point where acid and base have reacted in the correct mole ratio; the end point is the pH at which the chosen indicator visibly changes colour","The equivalence point and end point are two names for exactly the same thing in every titration","The end point always occurs before any acid or base has been added","The equivalence point is determined by the indicator alone, with no reference to stoichiometry"],explain:"The equivalence point is a stoichiometric/graphical concept (the exact mole-ratio point); the end point is the experimentally observed pH at which the indicator changes colour. A well-chosen indicator has its end point range bracket the true equivalence point."},
{ch:19,q:"What is a Lewis acid?",choices:["An electron-pair acceptor","An electron-pair donor","A proton donor only","A substance that always contains a releasable hydrogen"],explain:"A Lewis acid is defined as an electron-pair acceptor, broader than the Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry definition of a proton donor."},
{ch:19,q:"What is a Lewis base?",choices:["An electron-pair donor","An electron-pair acceptor","A proton acceptor only","A substance that must contain a hydroxide ion"],explain:"A Lewis base is defined as an electron-pair donor."},
{ch:19,q:"How does every Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid&ndash;base reaction relate to Lewis theory?",choices:["It is also a Lewis acid&ndash;base reaction, since the base donates a lone pair to form a new bond to H<sup>+</sup>, which accepts it","It is never classified as a Lewis acid&ndash;base reaction","Lewis theory and Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory describe completely unrelated, non-overlapping sets of reactions","Only strong acid&ndash;strong base reactions count as Lewis reactions"],explain:"Every Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry reaction is also a Lewis acid&ndash;base reaction, since the base donates its lone pair of electrons to H<sup>+</sup>, which accepts that pair to form a new bond."},
{ch:19,q:"Why can boron trifluoride, BF<sub>3</sub>, never act as a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid, yet it still acts as a Lewis acid towards NH<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["BF<sub>3</sub> has no hydrogen to donate as a proton, but boron has an incomplete octet and so can accept a lone pair of electrons from NH<sub>3</sub>","BF<sub>3</sub> has a releasable hydrogen but no lone pair","BF<sub>3</sub> always acts as a Lewis base, never as a Lewis acid","BF<sub>3</sub> and NH<sub>3</sub> do not react with each other at all"],explain:"BF<sub>3</sub> has no hydrogen, so it cannot be a Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acid; however, boron's incomplete octet (only six electrons) allows it to accept the lone pair donated by NH<sub>3</sub>, making it a Lewis acid in that reaction."},
{ch:19,q:"In the reaction BF<sub>3</sub> + :NH<sub>3</sub> &rarr; F<sub>3</sub>B&ndash;NH<sub>3</sub>, which species is the Lewis acid and which is the Lewis base?",choices:["BF<sub>3</sub> is the Lewis acid (electron-pair acceptor); NH<sub>3</sub> is the Lewis base (electron-pair donor)","NH<sub>3</sub> is the Lewis acid; BF<sub>3</sub> is the Lewis base","Both BF<sub>3</sub> and NH<sub>3</sub> act as Lewis acids simultaneously","Neither species is classified by Lewis theory, since no proton is transferred"],explain:"BF<sub>3</sub> accepts the electron pair donated by NH<sub>3</sub> into a new covalent bond, making BF<sub>3</sub> the Lewis acid and NH<sub>3</sub> the Lewis base."},
{ch:19,q:"In the formation of [Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> from Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) and NH<sub>3</sub>(aq), which species is the Lewis acid and which is the Lewis base?",choices:["Cu<sup>2+</sup>, with empty orbitals, is the Lewis acid; each NH<sub>3</sub> ligand, donating a lone pair, is a Lewis base","Cu<sup>2+</sup> is the Lewis base; NH<sub>3</sub> is the Lewis acid","Both species act purely as Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry acids in this reaction","Neither species can be classified by Lewis or Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory"],explain:"Cu<sup>2+</sup>, a metal cation with empty orbitals, accepts electron pairs and so is the Lewis acid; each NH<sub>3</sub> ligand donates a lone pair to form a dative covalent bond, acting as a Lewis base."},
{ch:19,q:"Why was a broader theory of acids and bases (Lewis theory) needed, beyond Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory?",choices:["Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory cannot classify reactions such as BF<sub>3</sub> + NH<sub>3</sub>, or the formation of transition metal complex ions, as acid&ndash;base reactions, since no proton changes hands in either case","Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory is entirely incorrect and should be discarded","Lewis theory was needed because Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory cannot explain any acid&ndash;base reaction involving water","Lewis theory replaces Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory completely, making the older theory obsolete"],explain:"Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory, based on proton transfer, cannot account for reactions with no proton transfer, such as BF<sub>3</sub> + NH<sub>3</sub> or complex ion formation; Lewis theory resolves this by focusing on the more fundamental process of electron-pair donation and acceptance."},
{ch:19,q:"What is the most fundamental process that both proton-transfer reactions and Lewis acid&ndash;base reactions with no proton transfer have in common?",choices:["The formation of a new covalent bond from a donated electron pair","The release of a gas as a product","A change in oxidation state of the central atom","The complete breaking of all bonds in the reactants"],explain:"Lewis theory identifies the common underlying process in both types of reaction as the formation of a new covalent bond using an electron pair donated by the base and accepted by the acid."},
{ch:19,q:"Why is it said that both Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory and Lewis theory have &ldquo;stood the test of time&rdquo;?",choices:["Each theory remains the right, well-defined tool for explaining a different range of acid&ndash;base behaviour, rather than one theory simply replacing the other","Only Lewis theory has survived, with Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory now considered entirely incorrect","Only Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory has survived, with Lewis theory now considered entirely incorrect","Neither theory is used in modern chemistry any longer"],explain:"Br&oslash;nsted&ndash;Lowry theory remains the natural framework for proton-transfer reactions, while Lewis theory extends acid&ndash;base concepts to reactions with no proton transfer; both are considered valid, each being the appropriate tool for its own well-defined range of chemistry."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: For a salt formed from a strong acid and a strong base (e.g. NaCl), what is the expected pH of its aqueous solution?",choices:["pH = 7, since neither the cation nor the anion hydrolyses appreciably","pH &gt; 7, since the cation hydrolyses","pH &lt; 7, since the anion hydrolyses","pH cannot be predicted for any salt solution"],explain:"Because neither ion of a strong acid/strong base salt hydrolyses appreciably in water, the resulting solution is neutral, with pH = 7."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which graph shape best describes a weak acid&ndash;weak base titration curve compared with the other three combinations?",choices:["A steady, smooth rise in pH with no sharp, near-vertical jump anywhere near the equivalence volume","An almost vertical jump of 8 pH units centred exactly on the equivalence point","A curve identical in every respect to the strong acid&ndash;strong base case","A curve that decreases in pH throughout, regardless of how much base is added"],explain:"The weak acid&ndash;weak base titration curve rises steadily and smoothly, with no large, sharp jump anywhere near the equivalence point, unlike the other three combinations."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Which statement correctly summarizes the sign of log<sub>10</sub>([salt]/[acid]) in the Henderson&ndash;Hasselbalch equation when [salt] &gt; [acid]?",choices:["Positive, so the buffer's pH is higher than pK<sub>a</sub>","Negative, so the buffer's pH is lower than pK<sub>a</sub>","Always exactly zero, regardless of the ratio","Undefined, since logarithms of ratios greater than 1 do not exist"],explain:"When [salt] &gt; [acid], the ratio [salt]/[acid] is greater than 1, so its logarithm is positive, making the buffer's pH higher than pK<sub>a</sub>."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: A buffer contains equal concentrations of NH<sub>3</sub> (K<sub>b</sub> = 1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) and NH<sub>4</sub>Cl. What is the pOH of this buffer at 298 K?",choices:["4.74","9.26","1.8","14.00"],explain:"pK<sub>b</sub> = &minus;log<sub>10</sub>(1.8 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>) = 4.74. Since [salt] = [base], pOH = pK<sub>b</sub> + log<sub>10</sub>(1) = 4.74."},
{ch:19,q:"HL: Using pOH = 4.74 for the NH<sub>3</sub>/NH<sub>4</sub>Cl buffer above, what is its pH at 298 K?",choices:["9.26","4.74","14.00","2.37"],explain:"pH = 14.00 &minus; pOH = 14.00 &minus; 4.74 = 9.26, consistent with this being a basic buffer (pH &gt; 7)."}
]
